# Fully derived Tremor outputs. All three are rewritten in full on every run, so
# keeping them in git means adding hundreds of megabytes per run without a single
# new fact - 4.9 GB a day once the pipeline runs hourly, which is what made this
# worth revisiting. Every one of them is restored by re-running the pipeline from
# the bars, which is what the hourly job does anyway before it reads them.
#
#   events/    - the event export of §6.5. The schema lives in
#                schema/event_export.schema.json and is versioned; the export
#                itself is produced by python -m tremor.export.
#   residuals/ - the SAED residual series, from python -m tremor.saed.
#   metrics/   - the per-asset metrics, from python -m tremor.pipeline. These
#                used to be tracked, on the reasoning that they cost a full
#                pipeline run to rebuild where the other two are seconds on top.
#                That was true of a pipeline run by hand a few times a month. It
#                is 157 MB rewritten twenty-four times a day now, and the rebuild
#                is 63 seconds.
data/tremor/events/
data/tremor/residuals/
data/tremor/metrics/

# The same reasoning, arrived at late and the hard way. These are single files
# rather than directories, so they looked small enough to keep - and they are
# not, because git cannot delta them. A parquet of floats that the pipeline
# recomputes in full has no bytes in common with its previous version, so every
# commit stores the WHOLE file again:
#
#     metrics_basket_hour.parquet   13 copies, 108.5 MB   8.3 MB a commit
#     decision_log.parquet          10 copies,  44.9 MB
#     detector_scores.parquet        1 copy,    25.2 MB
#     saed_events.parquet           14 copies,  13.8 MB
#
# against the bar archive, which appends and therefore deltas: a 2 MB parquet
# with one more hour in it costs 2.3 KB in the pack, measured. So the bars are
# not the problem and never were - these are, at about ten megabytes a day.
#
# Every one of them is rebuilt from the bars by the run that reads it, and the
# reading always happens in the SAME workflow run that wrote it (cluster reads
# what saed just wrote; delivery reads the events table the pipeline just
# produced). Nothing anywhere loads them from a previous run, so committing
# them buys nothing at all.
data/tremor/metrics_basket_hour.parquet
data/tremor/decision_log.parquet
data/tremor/detector_scores.parquet
data/tremor/detector_alerts.parquet
data/tremor/saed_events.parquet
data/tremor/saed_block_alerts.parquet
data/tremor/cluster_events.parquet
data/tremor/cluster_event_escalations.parquet
data/tremor/market_events.parquet
data/tremor/truth_labels.parquet
